@zz85 I have a few question that would help me a lot in fixing this:
1. From what I can gather, all of the getPoints switch logic was replaced with curves that have built-in getPoints, but there's a huge chunk of code at Path.prototype.getPoints that duplicates all of that logic still. What's the plan for this? Is it supposed to be gutted?
2. When arc was replaced with a subset of ellipse (equal radiuses), all of the arc code seems to have stayed but isn't used. As far as I can tell, it's not possible for an "ARC" action to even be added to a path. Shouldn't that just be gutted?

A big part of the problem which drove me crazy finding is here:

```
THREE.Path.prototype.absellipse = function ( aX, aY, xRadius, yRadius,
                                      aStartAngle, aEndAngle, aClockwise ) {

    var args = Array.prototype.slice.call( arguments );
    var curve = new THREE.EllipseCurve( aX, aY, xRadius, yRadius,
                                    aStartAngle, aEndAngle, aClockwise );
    this.curves.push( curve );

    var lastPoint = curve.getPoint(aClockwise ? 0 : 1);
    args.push(lastPoint.x);
    args.push(lastPoint.y);

    this.actions.push( { action: THREE.PathActions.ELLIPSE, args: args } );

 };
```

UGH. It does that aClockwise ? 0 : 1, when in fact the last point is always just 1 (at least for getSpacedPoints) because the curve already makes t = 1-t. But I bet that breaks getPoints. Still don't understand the history here. : /

Anyway, pretty close to having a PR.

@em I think you're right. I haven't used ellipse, but `THREE.EllipseCurve` does not seem to handle the clockwise flag correctly. I think this is a good time this issue is brought up since Chrome is also [implementing a ellipse function for canvas](http://www.rgraph.net/blog/2013/january/html5-canvas-ellipse.html) and we can rethink the API a little if needed.

Good observation about internal representation `THREE.Path` using actions vs curves. `Path` was supposed to be an object which resembles the 2d canvas path api, and it was initially implement with the actions. I added the curves representation thinking it might be a more useful data structure for perhaps a graphical tool for editing the path (and this reminds me of yet another another thing that should have been done)

Since Path extends CurvePath, it _might_ work by removing the current `Path.getPoints()` implementation, but I haven't tested that. Let me know if you have any suggestions as you're looking at this,

